Learning GitHub Actions – Day 10: Manual Triggers using workflow_dispatch
Continuing my GitHub Actions learning journey as part of my Cloud & DevOps roadmap!

📌 What is workflow_dispatch?
workflow_dispatch is an event trigger in GitHub Actions that allows us to start a workflow manually instead of waiting for an automatic event like a code push or pull request.
It is useful when we want to run specific tasks on demand, such as deploying an application, running tests, or performing maintenance activities.

🛠️ Practical Example:
name: Manual Workflow

  on:
   workflow_dispatch:

  jobs:
   demo:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
       steps: 
         - name: Display Message
           run: echo "Workflow triggered manually!"

         - name: Check Current Date
           run: date

🔍 How it works:
workflow_dispatch: Enables manual execution of the workflow.
jobs: Defines the tasks to be executed.
runs-on: Specifies the runner environment.
run: Executes shell commands.

How to trigger it manually:
Push the workflow file to your GitHub repository.
Navigate to the Actions tab.
Select the workflow.
Click Run workflow.
Select the branch and start the execution.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Manual triggers give us control over when workflows execute. They are especially useful for on-demand deployments, testing, and maintenance tasks without requiring a new code commit.
